As you are aware Vega Azul and IBM Power both allow some form of dcache zeroing. I forget
what their differences are.

I disagree with both approaches because they waste dcache lines for data that has constant value.

Instead I propose 
-> putting a bit in the page table that says the underlying page is zero no need
to fwd read request to dcache or L2 or main memory instead just return zero.

-> btw such reads should not allocate out of dcache 

-> on first write when you set the DIRTY bit also clear this bit

This should work on VIPT / VIVT dcaches essentially on a dcache miss you wait to hear back from
the TLB and based on the above response avoid allocating out of dcache.

==> Btw please note this is not about delayed zeroing of page rather saving dcache space. Of
course this uses page granularity instead of cache line granularity.

==> To avoid zero writes you could use zero pages and take an extra first write fault

